## 4. Root Cause

Program construction registers workflows from both workspaces into one flat Commander namespace,
with nothing checking for a name already taken:

| Step | File | What happens |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | `src/cli/agentic-hq-program.ts:40-45` | The built-in `list` subcommand is registered first. |
| 2 | `src/cli/agentic-hq-program.ts:48` | `searchResults.registerWorkflowsWith(new WorkflowRegistryImpl(program, builder))`. |
| 3 | `src/workflow-discovery/workflow-listing/workflow-search-results-impl.ts:44-47` | AHQ package registers **first**, then the current user workspace. |
| 4 | `src/workflow-discovery/workspace/current-user-workspace-impl.ts:44-49` | Skips itself **only** when `isAhqPackage()` — i.e. only when cwd *is* the package root. |
| 5 | `src/workflow-discovery/workspace/workspace-impl.ts:54-58` → `plugin/plugin-impl.ts:42-46` | Each plugin registers each discovered workflow, unconditionally. |
| 6 | `src/cli/workflow-registry-impl.ts:35-36` | `this.program.command(shortName)` — **Commander throws** on a duplicate name. |

The existing `isAhqPackage()` guard is a *same-directory* guard, not a *same-name* guard. It was
only ever designed to stop the AHQ package's own plugins being registered twice when you run the CLI
from inside the package. It cannot help when the two roots genuinely differ — which is precisely the
npm-installed case, and is now the **normal** case rather than the exception.

**Failure mode:** unrecoverable, uncaught, at construction time. That is consistent with this repo's
documented catastrophic-failure convention (throw uncaught, full stack trace, no logging system) —
but this is not a catastrophic failure. It is ordinary, user-caused, entirely foreseeable input, and
it should never have been allowed to reach that convention.

