## 6. The Design Decision (needs the human)

The Jira lists three candidates. Here is each one assessed against everything found above.

| Option | What it does | Assessment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **A. Local overrides package** | The local workspace's workflow wins; the package's copy is shadowed, with a notice. | Matches the mental model ("my project's stuff wins in my project") and matches how Claude Code's own plugin loading behaves. **But** per §5.2 it is only honest if `--plugin-dir` ordering is flipped to match; otherwise it announces local and runs the package's copy. Says nothing about §3.3 or §3.4. |
| **B. Qualified registration** | Both stay runnable under collide-free names (e.g. `my-local-plugin:add-feature`). | Nothing is lost and nothing is ambiguous, but it needs a naming scheme, changes the listing, and — because Claude itself collapses same-named plugins (§5.2) — the *qualified* form still cannot reach the shadowed copy when the plugin names also match. Solves less than it appears to. |
| **C. Graceful, actionable error** | Name the colliding workflow and both source workspaces, then exit cleanly. | Minimum bar and strictly better than a stack trace, but the CLI stays **unusable** from that directory — `list`, which is exactly what the user needs, still does not run. |

**Recommendation: A, hardened — a deterministic precedence policy applied consistently at every
layer, plus a visible notice, and never a crash.** Concretely:

1. **One name, one winner, chosen by an explicit precedence rule.** Local workspace beats AHQ
   package. Within a single workspace, first-discovered wins (plugin directory order). The built-in
   `list` always beats any workflow — it is the user's only way to see what happened.
2. **The winner is the same everywhere.** The `--plugin-dir` flag order (§5.2) is derived from the
   same policy, so the workflow the CLI advertises is the workflow Claude actually runs.
3. **Shadowing is announced, never silent.** A short notice on **stderr** at startup (stderr so it
   cannot corrupt `list`'s stdout output or any piped use), plus a marker on the shadowed entry in
   `agentic-hq list` so the listing explains itself.
4. **Startup never throws for this.** A name collision is ordinary user input, not a catastrophic
   failure, so it must not use the uncaught-throw convention.

This is strictly a superset of option C's value (the user gets a clear, actionable message naming
both sources) while keeping the CLI usable, and it covers all four reproduced variants rather than
only the reported one.

**Questions for the human before implementation starts** — see §10.

