## 7. Plan Of Action

TDD throughout: **RED → GREEN → REFACTOR → VERIFY**, one cycle per numbered step. No test is edited
between its first (failing) run and its post-implementation run.

### Step 0 — Confirm the policy (blocking)

Get §10 answered. Everything below assumes the recommended policy in §6; a different answer changes
Steps 2–5 but not Steps 1, 6, or 7.

### Step 1 — Failing test that reproduces the reported crash

A unit test over `createProgram` (or `WorkflowSearchResultsImpl`) with two stub workspaces both
offering `add-feature`. Asserts the program is constructed **and** exposes exactly one `add-feature`
subcommand. Currently throws `cannot add command 'add-feature'…` — a valid RED.
Home: `tests/unit/cli/agentic-hq-program.unit.test.ts` (extend) — the existing stub-based
`createStubSearchResults` helper there is already the right shape.

### Step 2 — Collision-aware registration (the core fix)

Give the registry the knowledge that names are unique. Candidate shape (Planner's call): a
`WorkflowRegistry` decorator or a value object owning the taken-name set, so `WorkflowRegistryImpl`
keeps its single responsibility ("turn a workflow into a Commander subcommand") and the *policy*
lives in one named place. Seed the taken-name set with the reserved `list` (§3.4) so the built-in
always wins. Registration order already gives local-last, so achieving *local wins* means either
reversing the two calls in `workflow-search-results-impl.ts:44-47` or making the policy explicit
about precedence rather than relying on call order — prefer the explicit version.

Covers §3.2, §3.3 and §3.4 with one mechanism.

### Step 3 — Make the winner real at the Claude layer (§5.2)

Failing test first: assert the `--plugin-dir` flags emitted by `ClaudeCommandBuilder` put the
winning workspace's plugin dir ahead of the losing one for a colliding plugin name. Then implement.
Without this step the fix is cosmetic for the flagship repo-clone case.

### Step 4 — Announce the shadowing

Notice on stderr naming the workflow, the winner's root and the shadowed one's root. Test asserts
the message names both roots (not its exact wording).

### Step 5 — Listing shows the outcome (§5.4)

Mark the shadowed entry in `agentic-hq list` so the listing explains itself. Test in
`tests/unit/cli/listing/listing-formatter.unit.test.ts`. Per the repo's convention on test intent,
assert the behavioural property (the shadowed entry is distinguishable and names its state), not the
literal glyph.

### Step 6 — Path normalisation decision, implemented (§5.5)

Normalise both sides of `WorkspaceImpl.isAhqPackage()`. Failing test first: a workspace whose
`rootDir` matches the package root except for a trailing separator must report `isAhqPackage() ===
true`. Record in the code comment that the symlink case was tested and needs nothing, so a future
reader does not re-investigate it.

### Step 7 — Regression proof at the real level

An integration/e2e test that runs the CLI from a fixture workspace containing a colliding workflow
and asserts a clean exit with a usable listing. `tests/e2e/demo/cross-workspace-list-workflows.e2e.test.ts`
is the closest template. **Note the known suite-contention issue** flagged in the AHQ-195 brief
(`publish-guards` + `build-determinism` both stage into `release/`) — do not add a third test that
races on `release/`.

### Step 8 — Verify AHQ-200's AC 5 at runtime (carried instruction)

During this ticket's own add-feature run, open Command 01's `command-input.json` and confirm it
reads `ahq-package-root=…`. Record the result in the implementation summary. If AHQ-205 is not run
through add-feature, carry the check forward to whichever ticket is.

### Step 9 — Validate and document

`pnpm validate` (typecheck + lint + format + unit) plus the relevant integration/e2e tests. Add the
collision policy to `docs/dev/how-agentic-hq-works.md` — no doc currently mentions collisions at all
(verified by grep across `docs/dev`, `docs/user-docs` and `src`), so this is new content, not an
edit.

